{"name":"Hair loss vaccine / peptides (BPC-157 for hair)","deep":true,"image":null,"mentions":219,"casual":{"lead":["BPC-157 is a 15-amino-acid synthetic peptide derived from a protein in human gastric juice. Online posters treat it and similar peptide stacks as an experimental route to scalp healing and thicker hair.","Animal studies suggest cytoprotective and anti-inflammatory effects and possible faster tissue healing. Its research is mostly in laboratory animals with very limited human data, and no human hair trial was found; how it would affect follicles has not been established."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["It circulates in online communities as a research peptide, injected or applied, often alongside standard hair treatments, with users reporting over weeks to months."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["It is not approved by any regulator for human use and has been on the WADA prohibited list since 2022. Health authorities discourage use because human safety data are insufficient, and because it promotes blood vessel growth there is concern it could affect undetected cancers."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[],"links":{"studies":[{"title":"Oral and topical peptides for skin aging: systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41924746/","source":"Frontiers in medicine","year":2026,"cites":null,"kind":"Systematic Review"},{"title":"Cosmeceuticals in photoaging: A review.","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39233460/","source":"Skin research and technology : official journal of International Society for Bioengineering and the Skin (ISBS) [and] International Society for Digital Imaging of Skin (ISDIS) [and] International Society for Skin Imaging (ISSI)","year":2024,"cites":null,"kind":"Systematic Review"},{"title":"Antimicrobial resistance crisis: could artificial intelligence be the solution?","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38254241/","source":"Military Medica